export const KNOWLEDGE = "# Breakeven\n\n> AI infrastructure cost, at your volume. Breakeven helps a company decide whether AI pays off for a piece of work, and whether to run it on a pay-per-token API, rented cloud GPUs, or GPUs it owns. All math runs in the browser from tested formulas and dated, sourced public prices. Built by Angel Ade-Oduntan (Columbia Business School, B8160 Vibe Coding capstone).\n\nLive: https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app\nContact: aoa2178@columbia.edu\nCode: https://github.com/aoa2178-alt/Angel-Vibe-Coding (folder breakeven/)\n\n## Pages (the path)\n\n- [Evaluate AI ROI](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/worth-it): the AI business case. People cost today against people plus AI compute: monthly savings, payback, 12-month ROI. A necessity test compares AI with rules automation and process redesign and returns Invest, Pilot, Defer, Reject or Don't use AI.\n- [Compare compute costs](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/run-it): monthly cost of the API, rented GPUs and owned GPUs at a volume, the volumes where the cheapest option changes, a scorecard beyond cost, and how busy GPUs can run.\n- [Power planning](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/power-it): only relevant when owning wins. Power needed and the cheapest way to bridge a late grid connection.\n- [Recommendation](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/result): the call, what would change it, and a 30/60/90 plan.\n- [Decision report](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/report): a one-page dashboard of the recommendation, printable.\n- [Sources](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/sources): every default with its range, source and confidence, plus price history.\n- [FAQ](https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/faq)\n\n## Build a link to a scenario\n\nEvery page reads the scenario from the URL, so an agent can open an exact case. Main parameters (all optional; anything missing uses the default):\n\n- `v`: AI tokens per month, in millions (default 2000 = 2B). Example: `v=30000` is 30B.\n- `t`: business-case template: `support`, `documents`, `sales` or `coding`.\n- `out`: share of tokens the AI writes, 0–1 (default 0.25).\n- `util`: how busy each GPU runs, 0–1 (default 0.6).\n- `now`: how the company runs AI today: `none`, `api`, `rent`, `own` or `mix`.\n- `mode=product`: an AI product you sell (freemium economics) instead of internal work.\n- Prices: `api_in`, `api_out` ($ per million tokens), `rent` ($ per GPU-hour), `hw` ($ per GPU), `dep` (years), `kw` (kW per GPU), `pue`, `kwh` ($ per kWh), `colo` ($ per kW-month), `support` (% of hardware a year), `tps` (tokens per second per GPU).\n- Report only: `report_name`, `report_now` (e.g. `Pay-per-token API`), `report_growth` (% a year), `report_years` (1–5).\n\nExamples:\n- Compare costs at 30B tokens a month: https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/run-it?v=30000\n- Customer-support business case, running on the API today: https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/worth-it?t=support&now=api\n- Decision report, 2B tokens, 60% growth over 3 years: https://breakeven-silk.vercel.app/report?t=support&v=2000&now=api&report_growth=60&report_years=3\n\n## How the numbers are calculated\n\n- GPUs needed = average tokens per second ÷ (GPU throughput × utilization), at least 1. Average tokens per second = monthly tokens ÷ (730 × 3600).\n- API cost = tokens × blended price, where blended = input price × (1 − output share) + output price × output share.\n- Rent cost = GPUs × 730 hours × $ per GPU-hour.\n- Own cost = GPUs, bought in whole 8-GPU servers, × (hardware ÷ depreciation months + support % of hardware ÷ 12 + kW × PUE × 730 × $ per kWh + kW × colocation $ per kW-month).\n- Business case: people cost today = tasks × minutes ÷ 60 × hourly cost. With AI: failed tasks redone by people + review time + AI compute at the cheapest option. Savings = the difference; payback = setup cost ÷ monthly savings.\n- The recommendation weighs cost with data control, time to launch, model quality, ease of running and flexibility (0–5 weights).\n\n## Defaults (October 2026, illustrative, all editable)\n\nAPI $2 input / $10 output per million tokens (Claude Sonnet 5.5 list price); rental $2.50 per H100-hour; hardware $35,000 per GPU all-in; 1.3 kW per GPU; PUE 1.3; electricity $0.10 per kWh; colocation $195 per kW-month; support 10% a year; 4-year depreciation; 1,500 tokens per second per GPU.\n\nAt these defaults the API is cheapest below about 460M tokens a month, renting is cheapest in between, and owning is cheapest for good from about 43B tokens a month.\n\n## Notes for agents\n\n- Figures are estimates for planning, not provider quotes. \"Rent\" and \"Own\" assume running a comparable open-weight model; frontier models such as Claude and GPT are API-only.\n- Breakeven has no accounts and stores nothing. The calculations run in the browser; the only data sent anywhere is a question typed into the \"Questions?\" panel, which goes with a summary of the scenario on screen to Claude (Anthropic) to answer.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Prices and assumptions\n\nQ: What do the default assumptions represent?\nA: Illustrative October 2026 values, not quotes. API pricing uses Claude Sonnet 5.5 list prices ($2 input and $10 output per million tokens). Rental uses about $2.50 per H100-hour on GPU-focused clouds (market average about $3.60; hyperscalers about $7). Hardware is about $35,000 per GPU, from 8-GPU H100 servers at $250–320K, drawing 1.3 kW per GPU including its share of the server. Throughput is a conservative estimate for a 70B-class open-weight model with batching. Every value is editable; the Sources page gives ranges and references.\n\nQ: Are the prices current?\nA: API and GPU rental prices are tracked over time on the Sources page. When the market moves more than 5% from your assumptions, the cost comparison offers to apply current prices. A weekly review proposes updates, and nothing changes without approval.\n\nQ: What is excluded from the estimates?\nA: Taxes, data egress, setup and change-management costs. All figures are illustrative estimates, not provider quotes.\n\n### Cost comparison\n\nQ: Why not just use the API?\nA: At low volume the API is the right call: you pay only for what you use and run nothing yourself. As volume grows, dedicated GPUs get cheaper per token. At the default assumptions, renting cloud GPUs costs less above about 460 million tokens a month, and owning costs least for good above about 43 billion. Compare compute costs shows where those points fall for your own assumptions.\n\nQ: What if our volume doubles?\nA: Change the volume on Compare compute costs (the calculator keys or dial) and every number updates, including the recommendation. The decision report also asks for expected yearly growth and shows the cost, and the cheapest option, for each year of your horizon.\n\nQ: Why do Rent and Own assume an open-weight model?\nA: Frontier models such as Claude and GPT are available only through their providers' APIs. Rent and Own therefore mean running an open-weight model of similar size on your own capacity, so switching is a quality decision as well as a cost decision.\n\nQ: How is the number of GPUs calculated?\nA: Average tokens per second, divided by what one GPU serves at the target utilization, rounded up. Rented GPUs are added one at a time; owned GPUs are bought in whole 8-GPU servers.\n\nQ: Why can renting and owning trade places as volume grows?\nA: Owned GPUs come in whole 8-GPU servers, so each new server is partly idle until volume fills it, while rented GPUs are added one at a time. Between the two crossover points the lowest-cost option can alternate.\n\nQ: What does the sensitivity analysis test?\nA: Each cost driver is moved 25% lower and 25% higher, one at a time. Drivers that change the lowest-cost option are listed first; the rest are ranked by how much they move cost.\n\nQ: What is PUE?\nA: Power usage effectiveness: total facility power divided by the power the computing equipment uses. A PUE of 1.3 adds 30% for cooling and other facility load.\n\n### Operations\n\nQ: How is response time modelled?\nA: As a queue: wait = variability × utilization × service time, using the Kingman approximation for a single GPU and the Sakasegawa extension for a fleet. Larger fleets pool their traffic, so they can run at higher utilization for the same wait. Real serving batches requests; the model is illustrative, but the shape of the curve holds.\n\nQ: When should capacity be owned rather than rented at peak?\nA: Own a GPU only if it is busy for more of the day than its critical ratio: its monthly ownership cost divided by the cost of renting it on demand for a full month. Always-busy base capacity is usually cheaper to own; peak capacity is usually cheaper to rent.\n\nQ: Why is owned hardware treated as a sunk cost?\nA: Once purchased, the hardware cost is not recovered by keeping it running. Retention therefore compares running cost only (support, power and space) with the cost of renting equivalent capacity, which falls each year. Resale value would bring retirement forward.\n\n### Business case and power\n\nQ: Why compare AI with non-AI options?\nA: Many workflow problems are solved more cheaply by redesigning the process or by rules-based automation. Measuring AI against those options, and against no change, keeps the recommendation honest: the test can conclude that AI is not the right investment.\n\nQ: How is the AI decision made?\nA: Each option is valued on cash benefit over three years at a 10% discount rate, in a conservative, base and optimistic case. If a non-AI option creates more value in the base case, the decision is Don't use AI. Otherwise: Invest if AI stays positive in the conservative case, Pilot if it is positive only in the base case, Defer if only the optimistic case pays, and Reject if none does. Inputs still at template defaults are listed as evidence to confirm. The conservative case lowers the AI resolution rate by 15 points and raises review time 50%, compute 25% and one-time costs 30%, with rules coverage ×0.75 and redesign effect ×0.5; the optimistic case moves them the other way (+10 points, −25%, −20%, −10%, ×1.15, ×1.25).\n\nQ: What is the difference between cash savings and capacity?\nA: Freed hours only become cash when they avoid hiring, overtime or contractors, or reduce headcount through attrition. The cash share sets how much of the freed time counts as savings; the rest is shown as capacity redeployed, in hours and FTEs, and is excluded from the decision.\n\nQ: Why does compute matter less than the resolution rate?\nA: In most workflows the remaining human effort, escalations and review, costs far more than the tokens. Raising the AI resolution rate or cutting review time usually moves savings more than changing the deployment model.\n\nQ: Can batteries power a site without a grid connection?\nA: No. Batteries extend a partial, curtailable connection by covering curtailment events. Bridging costs are measured as incremental spend versus owning the cluster on a working grid from day one; until an option is ready, the cluster is rented.\n\nQ: When is power not a constraint?\nA: Below about 1 MW, a colocation cage can usually supply the power today. Grid connection delays become material at multi-megawatt scale.";
